From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MilkFlint <milkflint@localhost>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net/sched: sch_cake: cake_mq: share the rate by demand and
 weight

cake_mq gave each active sub-cake rate / n, counted every sync_time
(200 us). A sub-cake that can't use its share can't lend it, and the
split follows the number of busy tx queues, not the flows in them: with
80% of the traffic on one queue and 20% on another, both get half and
the total is 70% (Netdevconf 0x19's own case); one flow on a queue gets
as much as seven on another; with host isolation, a host's share
depends on how many queues its flows hash to. Spreading flows over the
queues (RSS, an ifb flow hash, XPS) then costs throughput and fairness.

Each sub-cake now publishes, at its resync, what it uses (an average of
the bytes charged to its shaper, all of them and its sparse flows'
part), its bulk weight, and whether it wants more than its share. A
busy sub-cake reads the others that are active and takes

	share = sparse + (rate - used) * bid / (bid + saturated bids)

- An unsaturated sub-cake keeps what it uses: that comes off the rate
  ("used"), and the others share out the rest, so a share one can't
  use goes to those that can.
- The saturated ones split what's left by bid, after each one's sparse
  flows, which go first as in one CAKE. A sub-cake bids its bulk
  weight: each flow in its bulk rotation weighs 65535 / its host load,
  set at each refill of its deficit, which is how one CAKE's DRR shares
  the rate out. A sub-cake with no bulk flow bids as one host's only
  flow, so it can grow into a share.
- Saturated means it used 31/32 of its share, or 7/8 with its shaper
  holding packets back lately: a TCP flow held back by its share
  averages 85-95% of it, while a stream below its share never waits.
- Never above the rate, and never below what it would get if every
  sub-cake wanted more (its sparse flows plus its bid's part of what
  everyone's sparse flows leave), which holds it up while another's
  average catches up with that one slowing down.

The shares add up to the rate, give or take how far the averages lag,
and the flows get what one CAKE would give them, wherever they're
queued. Only a move of more than 1/256 redoes the tins' rates.

Each sub-cake has its own time scale: four of its packet intervals,
from 2 ms to 100 ms. Its usage is averaged over it, and with no packet
queued it stays active that long after its last one. A flow of
5 Mbit/s sends a packet every 2.4 ms, often with nothing queued in
between, and counting only a sub-cake that sent within the last resync
(200 us) left most such flows out of the others' sums. Counting one
after it stops holds back at most four packets' worth.

A new share also applies to what the shaper still waits for, both
ways: a packet sent at a small share could otherwise hold a sub-cake
for seconds once its share recovered, and one sent at a larger share
would let a share that moves up and down send at the higher one. A
sub-cake waiting for its shaper wakes at least every 2 ms to work its
share out again.

With host isolation, a host's flows can be in several sub-cakes. The
host loads now come from all of them: cake_mq keeps, for each tin, a
table of bulk flows per host tag (source and destination), and each
sub-cake mirrors every change of its own counts into it, atomically.
A host slot taken over with flows still counted moves them to the new
tag's bucket, as it does locally. In the host-only modes (srchost,
dsthost, hosts) each queue is a host's, and the table counts each
tag's bulk queues instead. Hosts whose tags meet in a bucket (1024 per
tin and side) are counted together while both have bulk flows. Plain
cake has no table and counts as before.

So that an idle sub-cake doesn't keep its flows in those loads, one
that has sent nothing for its time scale retires its bulk rotation, as
its next dequeue would (its watchdog wakes it); so do a reset and a
reconfigure that drops tins. A flow between bursts stays bulk, as in
one CAKE.

Replacing a cake_mq with another multi-queue qdisc destroys it before
its sub-cakes, which stay on the tx queues until the new qdisc's attach
puts them. cake_mq_destroy() now gives each of them a private copy of
the config, as plain cake, before freeing the shared config's table.

KUnit, 17 new cases (27 in all): the share alone, by weight (one flow
against seven with one server, two hosts, the 80/20 imbalance), lent
by unsaturated sub-cakes, with sparse flows, its floor, any rate and
usage at random; the usage average; saturation; the time scale; the
shaper's wait rescaled and the wake; 200 random mixes of idle,
held-back and saturated sub-cakes resyncing every 200 us, which settle
on weighted max-min shares within 2% of the rate; a host's flows over
two sub-cakes giving one host load, the host-only modes, a retag, the
retire on idle with the watchdog, and 3000 random flow changes over
four sub-cakes keeping the table equal to their counts. The 760-07
count cases follow the new activity rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
